          The "fully loaded" part is where the real cost hides. Recruiting alone averages about $4,700 per hire (SHRM). Then there's onboarding, weeks of training on your workflows, and the management time you spend supervising. And when a good VA leaves, and they do, you pay the recruiting and retraining bill all over again. Industry data puts the fully loaded annual cost of a full-time assistant in the $67,000-$109,000 range once benefits and overhead are counted.
        </p>
        <p style="color:#cbd5e1;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          And even at that price, a human VA is only human. They work business hours, take weekends and vacations, and call in sick. The lead that comes in at 9pm on a Sunday waits until Monday. That's not a knock on VAs, it's just the reality of hiring a person. Where a human genuinely wins is the nuance: a great VA builds real rapport, reads emotional cues, and handles relationship-heavy conversations with a warmth no AI can match.
        </p>
